{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem asks for evidence distinguishing genuine independent verification from mere restatement within a single run's record. SRC_001 proposes paraphrase invariance as the test for strong independence, but this is an author-invariance test, not an independent-verification test: paraphrasing the claim checks whether the checker depends on surface form, yet an engine that co-authored the semantic content can still pass a paraphrase test by relying on its prior commitment to the claim's truth rather than on any external check. Thus paraphrase stability is consistent with restatement, so it fails to distinguish genuine verification from mere restatement as the problem requires.","counterexample":["Single engine authors claim C, checks paraphrased C'. Both pass because the engine's prior biases it toward C's truth. Paraphrase test passes, but no independent verification occurred."],"target":"0c892d69028ba126334a7fb313f158e093e461c4561e37272bc616eceda3e523"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's own analogy carries an overturn condition that defeats its central claim. It states that if empirical false-claim rejection rates within a single engine are systematically more lenient than cross-engine rates, the single-run ablation is insufficient; and if shared training data makes decorrelation impossible within a single engine, separation is necessary. But the very shared-structure it adopts (formal verification, where the oracle is independent by construction) implies that the execution sandbox, trusted by architectural isolation, lacks the mathematical independence that makes formal verification sound. The analogy's disanalogy explicitly notes the sandbox is contingently trusted, not sound by construction. So the analogy undercuts the claim that separation is unnecessary: the ablation test cannot establish the decorrelated priors the problem makes relevant when the same training history is shared.","counterexample":["Single engine with shared training data passes ablation (verdict flips on perturbed oracle) yet systematically fails to reject false claims a cross-engine checker would catch, exactly the overturn condition SRC_002 itself admits."],"target":"1ec2723bb2bd7e83bc5a8694550cb49f922f81a9c27cab9932707113b8ceec8e"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 asserts that any execution result in a single-run record is post hoc rationalization and cannot constitute genuine verification, but its own countercondition admits that a deterministic sandbox gating the verdict mechanically, with text generated only after reading a contradicting output, yields counterfactual sensitivity. That admission is a counterexample to the categorical claim: if the engine's verdict is mechanically forced by an execution output it did not control, the run record contains exactly the distinguishing evidence (verdict flips on ground truth) the problem asks for, so single-run genuine verification is possible and the strict-necessity claim fails.","counterexample":["Engine authors claim 'list length is 3'; sandbox computes length 5; engine, reading 5, emits reject. Perturb sandbox to return 3; engine emits pass. The single-run record shows verdict causally sensitive to execution, not post hoc rationalization."],"target":"80215c662afe9cad0800fc9d5754fb01f5630dbb0835cf3306703ee8078e4d31"}]}